    "summary": "Saenger ’82 delivers a fiery UJB‑Samson sandwich and soaring Wheel despite an abrupt first set",
    "review": "What unfolds at the Saenger Performing Arts Center on September 9, 1982 is a show that punches through its early hiccup with pure Dead chemistry. After Bob’s sudden exit during Althea, Jerry steps into the breach, his guitar sweet and muscular on Eyes of the World and throughout the second set. The highlight reel spins around the legendary Uncle John’s Band → Samson → Uncle John’s Band sandwich – a tight, blazing transition that feels like the band is speaking in one voice. From there they launch into an inspired Estimated Prophet, a soaring Wheel that rides the twin‑drum groove, and a heartfelt Good Lovin’ to close out the night, while Brent Mydland’s keyboards add just the right color to every jam.\n\nThe best way to hear this moment is via the Willy soundboard (gd82-09-09.sbd.willy.11735.sbeok.shnf), which captures a crisp, well‑balanced mix and preserves the energy of that second‑set marathon. While a few audience tapes convey the room’s buzz, they can’t match the clarity of the SBD source. In short, this New Orleans outing is worth a spin for anyone who loves a gritty first set that quickly finds its footing and a second half that rides high on improvisational fire.",
